Top 50 Linux commands every DevOps engineer should know.

Whether you are debugging a broken deployment or securing your servers, mastering the Linux terminal is necessary. I've broken down the 50 essential commands by their missions:

→ 𝗦𝘆𝘀𝘁𝗲𝗺 𝗵𝗲𝗮𝗹𝘁𝗵 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗺𝗼𝗻𝗶𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴
1. uptime: Check server load averages.
2. free: Monitor RAM usage and check 'available' memory.
3. df: Track disk usage. 100% means apps will crash.
4. top: Live monitor for processes.
5. htop: A colorful, user-friendly version of top.
6. vmstat: Get a snapshot of CPU, memory, and IO.
7. iostat: View detailed disk I/O metrics.

→ 𝗛𝗶𝘀𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀
8. dmesg: Read kernel messages for OOM kills or segfaults.
9. journalctl: View the systemd master log.
10. sar: Review historical stats.
11. systemctl: Control and check the status of services.
12. ps aux: See all running processes.

→ 𝗟𝗼𝗴𝘀 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗳𝗶𝗹𝗲𝘀
13. grep: Search text and find patterns.
14. sed: Stream editor for in-place text replacement.
15. awk: Extract columns from text output.
16. find: Locate files by name, type, or size.
17. du: Check directory sizes.
18. xargs: Pipe outputs to commands.

→ 𝗡𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸 𝗱𝗶𝗮𝗴𝗻𝗼𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗰𝘀
19. curl / wget: Test HTTP requests and timing.
20. ss: Check socket stats and open ports.
21. netstat: The legacy version of ss.
22. dig: Troubleshoot DNS lookups.
23. ip addr: View IP configuration and interface status.
24. ifconfig: Legacy IP configuration.
25. mtr: Combine traceroute and ping to find network loss.
26. tcpdump: Capture packets for detailed analysis.
27. nmap: Scan for open, closed, or filtered ports.

→ 𝗨𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘀 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗽𝗲𝗿𝗺𝗶𝘀𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀
28. id: Show user and group IDs.
29. usermod: Modify users. 
30. groupmod: Rename a group while keeping members intact.
31. visudo: Safely edit /etc/sudoers with syntax checking.
32. chmod / chown: Change permissions and file ownership.
33. umask: Set default permissions for new files.

→ 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗲𝘀
34. sshd: Configure SSH access, like disabling root login.
35. iptables: Set firewall rules top-to-bottom.
36. kill: Send signals like SIGTERM (-15) or SIGKILL (-9).
37. pkill / pgrep: Kill or find processes by their name.
38. nice / renice: Adjust process priority.
39. lsof: List open files or ports.
40. strace: Trace system calls and signals.

→ 𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗮𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗺𝗮𝘁𝗲
41. tar: Archive and compress files.
42. rsync: Sync files across environments.
43. tmux: Keep terminal sessions alive if SSH dies.
44. crontab: Schedule recurring jobs.
45. logrotate: Auto-manage log files to prevent disks from filling up.

→ 𝗦𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗮𝗴𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗱𝗲𝗲𝗽 𝘀𝘆𝘀𝘁𝗲𝗺
46. lsblk: List block devices and their mount points.
47. fdisk: List and manage partitions.
48. mount: Make partitions accessible to the system.
49. chroot: Change the root directory.
50. ldd: Show shared libraries for a binary.
